    {
      "object_id": "mathia_interpretation_a82522884e71a036fd13586edea223e6823e1ade128a67163c43e1fdcad1ba5e",
      "object_role": "interpretation",
      "proposed_content": "Source fact: the high-frequency complex Gaussian field is represented by a one-sided Brownian Fourier integral. Its exponential covariance is analytically continued to a boundary Cauchy-type singularity; Plemelj and L2 boundedness of the Hilbert transform then give uniform second moments and negative-Sobolev martingale limits. Interpretation: the proof exploits cancellation encoded by analytic boundary values instead of attempting absolute kernel domination; positive-frequency support is the structural reason the nonintegrable singularity is manageable. Boundary: this is not a general construction for arbitrary complex log-correlated fields, and signs, contour side, stochastic normalization, and 2pi factors must be scan-checked.",
