    {
      "object_id": "mathia_interpretation_feda5dda90268f44d825dec31da9bf3287714429e545b0534c9dccaa4b66b6fb",
      "object_role": "interpretation",
      "proposed_content": "Source fact: the appendix bounds W1 by local Fourier-density discrepancy and tails, smooths a normalized independent sum with a small Gaussian, compares characteristic functions, and realizes a covariance-matched Gaussian coupling. Nearly degenerate covariance directions are projected away and bounded separately; concentration upgrades mean coupling error to exponential bounds, with all-lambda and quadratic-exponential improvements for uniformly bounded summands. Interpretation: smoothing makes distributional discrepancy accessible to Fourier inversion, while the eigenspace split avoids unstable inversion of an ill-conditioned covariance. Boundary: rates are deliberately nonoptimal and depend on fixed dimension and uniform moment hypotheses; the strongest tails require bounded summands.",
