    {
      "object_id": "mathia_synthesis_2298d1130f3b2746afde4d1c72155665dcbd1ed39eda506782675e65d77afad1",
      "object_role": "synthesis",
      "proposed_content": "A quantitative block coupling decomposes the randomized Euler-product logarithm into a log-correlated Gaussian field and a smoothly convergent error with exponential derivative bounds. That common decomposition supports three different passages: an L2 Hilbert-space exponential martingale gives complex distributional chaos; Gaussian-chaos Lp bounds plus comparable true normalizers give nontrivial subcritical real measures; and at criticality a separate bounded/off-diagonal covariance comparison with a known white-noise approximation, followed by careful smooth-factor transfer, supplies the corrected critical measure.",
