    {
      "object_id": "mathia_synthesis_e555c77df324628dff19db21e61f29e1226c4655bc05e76fdb0e62e244bb1927",
      "object_role": "synthesis",
      "proposed_content": "Pole subtraction first turns the boundary zeta kernel into a distributional arithmetic sampling norm; the pure and mixed shifted second moments then turn that norm into an Euler-product approximation metric. Integrating those fixed-test estimates over Fourier probes lifts the approximation to mean square in W^{-alpha,2}, where phase equidistribution and a Hilbert-space martingale identify the random Euler-product limit. The later multiplicative identity zeta_rand=e^E nu then resolves that same limit into a smooth multiplier and complex Gaussian chaos.",
